nodecode-oathkeeper is a rewrite of Whydiriki700's Whydiriki700/oathkeeper:

  https://github.com/Whydiriki700/oathkeeper
  commit dfff809521c78e98e461ce72d4592ee02eea345c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Riki Wahyudi (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the oath file and its three line kinds (a standing mission, a
`never <verb>: <pattern>` invariant, an `always require <evidence> before
<verb>: <pattern>` prerequisite), the path patterns with ** trees, globs and
prefix subtrees, the parsing and its per-line errors, the action extraction
from a shell command (rm, mv, cp, dd, truncate, tee, in-place editors,
redirections, mutating git verbs, over a bounded head), the ledger of blocks
and evidence, and the fingerprint snapshots with their outside-change diff.
The plugin's Python is now Lisp: oath.lisp is oath.py, actions.lisp is
actions.py, cell.lisp is __init__.py.

Left out: Hermes gave the plugin three hooks around a tool call; this
harness gives one around hook on the :TOOL point, so the pre-call check and
the post-call bookkeeping are one function and the fingerprint report runs at
the head of each call instead of at the head of each turn. The plugin's
pre_llm_call briefed the missions into the first turn's prompt; here they
stand in the cell's notice, which the model reads at the tail of every
round. The plugin read a command string from a terminal tool; this harness's
agent works through one eval form, so the extraction reads the form's spawn
calls and the file verbs it calls (delete-file, write-file, edit),
and its `oath_check` tool takes that form rather than a tool name and a JSON
argument object. Content fingerprints are a 64-bit FNV-1a digest rather than
SHA-256, to need no system beyond the core's own.
